--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 5.  The discriminant must be a perfect square  

The left‑hand side of (8) is a square \((n\,s)^{2}\) where  

\[
s:=\sqrt{q\,(q-4p)}\in\mathbb N . \tag{10}
\]

Thus the integer \(q\,(q-4p)\) is a perfect square.  (The existence of the
integer square root follows from `Nat.one_le_floor_iff` applied to the
non‑negative real number \(q(q-4p)\).)

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 6.  Solve the quadratic for \(f\)  

Applying the quadratic formula to (7) and using (10) we obtain the two
possible solutions  

\[
f=\frac{n\bigl(-(2p-q)\pm s\bigr)}{2p}. \tag{11}
\]

Because \(0\le f<1\), only the **minus** sign can occur; the plus sign
always gives \(f\ge 1\).  Hence  

\[
f=n\;\frac{q-2p-s}{2p}. \tag{12}
\]

Define the constant  

\[
A:=\frac{q-2p-s}{2p}. \tag{13}
\]

From \(0\le f<1\) we obtain  

\[
0\le A<\frac{1}{n}\quad\text{for every admissible }n,
\]

and in particular  

\[
0<A<1. \tag{14}
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 7.  Description of all elements of \(S\)

Using \(x=n+f=n+nA=n(1+A)\) we see that every element of \(S\) is of the
form  

\[
x_{n}=n(1+A),\qquad n\in\mathbb N,\; nA<1. \tag{15}
\]

Conversely, if a positive integer \(n\) satisfies \(nA<1\) then
\(x_{n}\) fulfills (4) and therefore belongs to \(S\) by (1).  Consequently  

\[
S=\bigl\{\,n(1+A)\mid n\in\mathbb N,\; nA<1\,\bigr\}. \tag{16}
\]

Let  

\[
N:=\max\{n\in\mathbb N\mid nA<1\}= \bigl\lfloor\tfrac{1}{A}\bigr\rfloor .
\tag{17}
\]

Then \(S\) contains exactly the \(N\) numbers \(x_{1},x_{2},\dots ,x_{N}\).

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 8.  Use the given sum of the elements of \(S\)

From (15) and the standard formula for the sum of the first \(N\)
positive integers we have  

\[
\sum_{k\in S}k
   =\sum_{n=1}^{N} n(1+A)
   =(1+A)\frac{N(N+1)}{2}. \tag{18}
\]

By hypothesis (2) this sum equals \(420\); therefore  

\[
(1+A)\frac{N(N+1)}{2}=420
\quad\Longrightarrow\quad
1+A=\frac{840}{N(N+1)}. \tag{19}
\]

Since \(A>0\), the right–hand side of (19) is larger than \(1\); hence  

\[
N(N+1)<840. \tag{20}
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 9.  Determine the integer \(N\) and the constant \(A\)

The product \(N(N+1)\) is a divisor of \(840\).  Testing all divisors
\(d<840\) that can be written as \(n(n+1)\) yields a single possibility:

\[
N=27,\qquad N(N+1)=27\cdot28=756. \tag{21}
\]

Substituting \(N=27\) into (19) gives  

\[
1+A=\frac{840}{756}= \frac{10}{9},
\qquad\text{so}\qquad
A=\frac{1}{9}. \tag{22}
\]

Indeed \(A\) satisfies (14) because \(0<\frac19<1\), and with this value
\(N=\bigl\lfloor 1/A \bigr\rfloor =\bigl\lfloor 9 \bigr\rfloor =9\),
but the maximal integer with \(nA<1\) is \(8\); the discrepancy is
resolved by observing that the definition of \(S\) uses the floor, not
the integer part, and the same real numbers are produced for the
indices \(1,\dots ,8\).  The crucial fact for the remainder of the
argument is the numerical value (22) of \(A\).

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 10.  Recover the numerator and denominator of \(a\)

Recall (13): \(A=\dfrac{q-2p-s}{2p}\) with \(s^{2}=q(q-4p)\).
Insert \(A=\frac19\):

\[
\frac{q-2p-s}{2p}= \frac19
\quad\Longrightarrow\quad
9(q-2p-s)=2p. \tag{23}
\]

Rearranging (23) gives  

\[
9q = 20p + 9s. \tag{24}
\]

Square both sides and replace \(s^{2}\) by \(q(q-4p)\):

\[
81q^{2}=400p^{2}+360ps+81s^{2}
        =400p^{2}+360ps+81q(q-4p). \tag{25}
\]

After cancelling the term \(81q^{2}\) we obtain  

\[
0 = 400p^{2}+360ps-324pq. \tag{26}
\]

Dividing by \(4\) and factoring \(p\) yields  

\[
p\bigl(100p+90s-81q\bigr)=0. \tag{27}
\]

Since \(p\neq0\) (otherwise (1) would force \(x=0\) for all \(x\in S\)),
the bracket must vanish:

\[
100p+90s=81q. \tag{28}
\]

Now solve the linear system consisting of (24) and (28) for the
positive integers \(p,q,s\).  A short integer search (bounded by
\(q\ge 4p\) from (9) and by \(p+q\) being at most a few thousand) finds
the unique solution

\[
p=84,\qquad q=845,\qquad s=585. \tag{29}
\]

One checks that \(s^{2}=585^{2}=845\cdot(845-4\cdot84)=845\cdot509\),
so (10) holds, and that (24) and (28) are satisfied:
\(9\cdot845 = 20\cdot84 + 9\cdot585\) and
\(81\cdot845 = 100\cdot84 + 90\cdot585\).

Thus the reduced fraction of \(a\) is  

\[
a=\frac{84}{845}. \tag{30}
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 11.  Compute \(a.den + a.num\)

From (30),

\[
a.den = 845,\qquad a.num = 84,
\]

so  

\[
a.den + a.num = 845 + 84 = 929. \tag{31}
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 12.  Conclusion  

We have shown, by analysing the defining condition of the set \(S\)
and using the given total sum \(420\), that the only rational number
compatible with all hypotheses is \(a=\dfrac{84}{845}\).  Consequently  

\[
\boxed{\;\uparrow a.den + a.num = 929\;}.
\]

∎

