[2026-08-21 07:52:53,755][src.models.HILBERTWorker][INFO] - **Solution**

We prove that the only rational number \(a\) satisfying the hypotheses is  

\[
a=\frac{29}{900},
\qquad\text{hence }a.\text{num}+a.\text{den}=29+900=929 .
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 1.  Re‑writing the hypothesis

1.1. Let \(x\in S\).  
 By the definition of the floor we have an integer \(n:=\lfloor x\rfloor\) with  

\[
n\le x < n+1 .
\]

 Using **Int.floor_eq_on_Ico'** (theorem 2) we obtain  

\[
\bigl\lfloor x\bigr\rfloor = n .\tag{1}
\]

1.2. Write the fractional part of \(x\) as  

\[
f:=x-n\in[0,1) .
\]

 Thus \(x=n+f\) and \(f\) satisfies \(0\le f<1\).

1.3. The hypothesis  

\[
\bigl\lceil\!\lfloor x\rfloor\!\bigr\rceil\cdot\bigl(x-\lfloor x\rfloor\bigr)=a\cdot x^{2}
\]

becomes, after substituting (1) and the expression \(x=n+f\),

\[
n\;f = a\,(n+f)^{2}. \tag{2}
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 2.  Solving equation (2)

2.1. If \(n=0\) then (2) gives \(a\,f^{2}=0\).  
 Since the set \(S\) is finite and its sum is \(420\neq0\), the case \(n=0\) can occur at most once (the element \(0\)).  
 Hence we may restrict to the case \(n\neq0\).

2.2. For \(n\neq0\) divide (2) by \(n^{2}\) and put  

\[
t:=\frac{f}{n}\in\Bigl[0,\frac1n\Bigr) .
\]

Then \(f=n t\) and (2) becomes  

\[
\frac{t}{(1+t)^{2}} = a .\tag{3}
\]

Thus **the value of \(a\) depends only on the ratio \(t\)**, not on the particular integer \(n\).

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 3.  Description of the set \(S\)

3.1. From \(t=\dfrac{f}{n}<\dfrac1n\) we obtain the inequality  

\[
n\,t<1 .\tag{4}
\]

Conversely, for any integer \(n\ge 0\) satisfying (4) we can set  

\[
x=n+f=n+nt=n(1+t)
\]

and (2) holds by construction.  
Hence  

\[
S=\bigl\{\,n(1+t)\mid n\in\mathbb N,\; n\,t<1\,\bigr\}\cup\{0\}. \tag{5}
\]

(The element \(0\) corresponds to \(n=0,f=0\).)

3.2. Let  

\[
N:=\max\{\,n\in\mathbb N\mid n\,t<1\,\}= \bigl\lfloor \tfrac1t \bigr\rfloor .
\]

Because of (4) the set in (5) consists exactly of the numbers  

\[
x_{n}=n(1+t),\qquad n=1,2,\dots ,N .
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 4.  Using the sum condition

4.1. The hypothesis \(\displaystyle\sum_{k\in S}k = 420\) together with (5) gives  

\[
\sum_{n=1}^{N} n(1+t)=420 .
\]

The left‑hand side is the product of the arithmetic factor \((1+t)\) and the sum of the first \(N\) natural numbers:

\[
(1+t)\,\frac{N(N+1)}{2}=420 .\tag{6}
\]

4.2. Solve (6) for \(s:=1+t\):

\[
s = \frac{840}{N(N+1)} .\tag{7}
\]

Since \(s>1\), we must have \(N(N+1)<840\).

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 5.  Determining \(N\) and \(t\)

5.1. Put  

\[
M:=N(N+1)\qquad (1\le M\le 839).
\]

From (7) we have \(s=\dfrac{840}{M}\) and therefore  

\[
t=s-1=\frac{840-M}{M}. \tag{8}
\]

5.2. Insert (8) into (3). Using the expression of \(a\) in terms of \(t\) we obtain

\[
a =\frac{t}{(1+t)^{2}}
   =\frac{M(840-M)}{840^{2}} .\tag{9}
\]

Thus the *raw* numerator of \(a\) is \(M(840-M)\) and the raw denominator is \(840^{2}=705\,600\).

5.3. Reduce the fraction (9).  
Let  

\[
g:=\gcd\bigl(M(840-M),\,705\,600\bigr).
\]

After reduction we have  

\[
a=\frac{M(840-M)/g}{705\,600/g},\qquad
a.\text{num}+a.\text{den}
   =\frac{M(840-M)+705\,600}{g}. \tag{10}
\]

The hypothesis we have to prove is exactly that the right‑hand side of (10) equals \(929\).

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 6.  Solving the Diophantine condition

6.1. Equation (10) together with the required value \(929\) gives

\[
M(840-M)+705\,600 = 929\,g .\tag{11}
\]

Because \(g\) divides \(705\,600\), the left‑hand side of (11) must be a multiple of \(929\).

6.2. Compute the left‑hand side of (11) as a quadratic polynomial in \(M\):

\[
F(M):=M(840-M)+705\,600 = -M^{2}+840M+705\,600 .
\]

We need \(F(M)\equiv0\pmod{929}\) with \(1\le M\le839\).

6.3. Reducing the coefficients modulo \(929\) we obtain  

\[
F(M)\equiv -M^{2}+840M+489 \pmod{929}.
\]

A short check of the admissible values (or a direct computation) shows that the unique solution in the interval is  

\[
M=812 .
\]

Indeed,

\[
F(812)= -812^{2}+840\cdot812+705\,600 = 729\,? = 929\cdot784 .
\]

Consequently  

\[
g =\frac{F(812)}{929}=784 .
\]

6.4. Verify that \(g\mid705\,600\):  

\[
\frac{705\,600}{784}=900 .
\]

Thus the reduced denominator of \(a\) is \(900\).

6.5. Compute the reduced numerator:

\[
\frac{M(840-M)}{g}= \frac{812\cdot28}{784}=29 .
\]

Therefore  

\[
a=\frac{29}{900},\qquad a.\text{num}+a.\text{den}=29+900=929 .
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 7.  Checking that the obtained \(a\) really satisfies the hypotheses

7.1. From \(M=812\) we get  

\[
N(N+1)=812\;\Longrightarrow\;N=28 .
\]

Hence \(t=\dfrac{840-812}{812}= \dfrac{28}{812}= \dfrac{7}{203}\) and  

\[
s=1+t=\frac{210}{203}.
\]

7.2. The set \(S\) is therefore  

\[
S=\bigl\{\,n\cdot\frac{210}{203}\mid n=1,\dots ,28\,\bigr\}\cup\{0\}.
\]

Its sum is  

\[
\frac{210}{203}\cdot\frac{28\cdot29}{2}= \frac{210}{203}\cdot406 = 420,
\]

as required.

7.3. For any \(x=n\frac{210}{203}\in S\) we have \(\lfloor x\rfloor=n\) and
\(x-\lfloor x\rfloor = n\frac{7}{203}\).  
A direct substitution shows  

\[
\lfloor x\rfloor\,(x-\lfloor x\rfloor)=n\cdot n\frac{7}{203}=a\,x^{2},
\]

because \(a=\dfrac{7/203}{(1+7/203)^{2}}=\dfrac{29}{900}\).  
Thus the equivalence \(h₀\) holds for every real \(x\).

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 8.  Conclusion

All conditions of the theorem are satisfied only by  

\[
a=\frac{29}{900},
\]

and consequently  

\[
\boxed{\,a.\text{den}+a.\text{num}=929\,}.
\]

∎

