[2026-08-21 07:55:52,608][src.models.HILBERTWorker][INFO] - <informal_proof>
**Goal.**  For a rational number `a` and a finite set `S : Finset ℝ` satisfying  

\[
\forall x\in\mathbb R,\;x\in S\iff \bigl(\!\uparrow\!\lfloor x\rfloor\bigr)\bigl(x-\!\uparrow\!\lfloor x\rfloor\bigr)=\!\uparrow a\;x^{2},
\tag{1}
\]

and  

\[
\sum_{k\in S}k = 420,
\tag{2}
\]

show that  

\[
\!\uparrow a.\text{den}+a.\text{num}=929 .
\tag{3}
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 1.  Re‑writing the defining equation

Let `x ∈ S`.  Put  

\[
n:=\lfloor x\rfloor\in\mathbb Z,\qquad
f:=x-n\in\mathbb R .
\]

By definition of the floor,  

\[
0\le f<1,\qquad x=n+f .
\tag{4}
\]

Equation (1) for this `x` becomes  

\[
n\;f = a\;(n+f)^{2}. \tag{5}
\]

Expanding the square and moving everything to the left gives a quadratic
equation in `f`:

\[
a f^{2}+(2an-n)f+an^{2}=0. \tag{6}
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 2.  The discriminant must be a rational square

The discriminant of (6) is  

\[
\Delta=n^{2}\bigl(1-4a\bigr). \tag{7}
\]

Because (6) has a real solution (`f`), we must have  

\[
1-4a\ge 0. \tag{8}
\]

All the data are rational, hence `1‑4a` is a rational number that admits a
real square root.  Consequently there exists a reduced fraction  

\[
\frac{r}{s}\in\mathbb Q,\qquad s>0,\; \gcd(r,s)=1,
\]

such that  

\[
1-4a=\Bigl(\frac{r}{s}\Bigr)^{2}. \tag{9}
\]

From (9) we obtain  

\[
a=\frac{1-\bigl(r/s\bigr)^{2}}{4}
   =\frac{s^{2}-r^{2}}{4s^{2}}. \tag{10}
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 3.  Solving the quadratic for the fractional part `f`

Using the quadratic formula on (6) and substituting the value of `a`
from (10) we get two possible solutions for `f`.  
The solution with the ‘‑’ sign is the only one that can satisfy `0≤f<1`
(because the ‘+’ sign would give `f≥1`).  A straightforward algebraic
simplification (using Lemma [Rat.den_mul_eq_num] to replace `a·den a` by
`num a`) yields

\[
f
   =\frac{1-2a-\frac{r}{s}}{2a}
   =\frac{(s-r)^{2}}{2s^{2}}\;\Big/\;
     \frac{s^{2}-r^{2}}{2s^{2}}
   =\frac{s-r}{s+r}. \tag{11}
\]

Thus for every `x∈S` we have  

\[
f=\frac{s-r}{s+r}\qquad\text{and}\qquad 
x=n+f=n\Bigl(1+\frac{s-r}{s+r}\Bigr)
   =n\;\frac{2s}{s+r}. \tag{12}
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 4.  Determining the admissible integers `n`

From (4) and (11),

\[
0\le n\frac{s-r}{s+r}<1
\;\Longleftrightarrow\;
0\le n<\frac{s+r}{s-r}. \tag{13}
\]

Hence the possible values of `n` are exactly  

\[
n=1,2,\dots ,N,
\qquad\text{where}\qquad
N:=\Bigl\lfloor\frac{s+r}{s-r}\Bigr\rfloor . \tag{14}
\]

Because `s` and `r` are coprime, the fraction \((s+r)/(s-r)\) is
already in lowest terms; consequently the floor equals the rational
value minus one, i.e.

\[
N=\frac{s+r}{s-r}-1=\frac{2r}{s-r}. \tag{15}
\]

In particular `s-r` divides `2r`, so `N` is an integer.

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 5.  The explicit description of `S` and its sum

From (12) and (14) we obtain the precise description

\[
S=\Bigl\{\,n\;\frac{2s}{s+r}\;\Big|\;n=1,\dots ,N\Bigr\}. \tag{16}
\]

Using the standard formula for the sum of the first `N` integers
(Lemma [Finset.sum_range]), together with Lemma [Finset.sum_apply'] to
evaluate the sum of a constant multiple, we compute

\[
\begin{aligned}
\sum_{x\in S}x
   &=\frac{2s}{s+r}\sum_{n=1}^{N}n
    =\frac{2s}{s+r}\cdot\frac{N(N+1)}{2}
    =\frac{s\;N(N+1)}{s+r}. \tag{17}
\end{aligned}
\]

By hypothesis (2) this sum equals `420`; inserting the expression
(15) for `N` gives

\[
\frac{s}{s+r}\;
   \frac{2r}{s-r}\Bigl(\frac{2r}{s-r}+1\Bigr)
   =420. \tag{18}
\]

A short rational simplification (multiply numerator and denominator by
\((s-r)^{2}\)) transforms (18) into the integer equation

\[
r\,s = 210\,(s-r)^{2}. \tag{19}
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 6.  Solving the Diophantine equation

Put \(d:=s-r>0\).  Then \(s=r+d\) and (19) becomes

\[
r(r+d)=210\,d^{2}
\;\Longleftrightarrow\;
r^{2}+rd-210d^{2}=0. \tag{20}
\]

Viewing (20) as a quadratic in `r` and using the integer discriminant
\(\Delta = d^{2}+840d^{2}=841d^{2}= (29d)^{2}\), we obtain the unique
positive solution

\[
r=\frac{-d+29d}{2}=14d,\qquad
s=r+d=15d. \tag{21}
\]

Thus `s` and `r` are proportional to `15` and `14` respectively.

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 7.  Computing `a`

Insert (21) into (10):

\[
a=\frac{s^{2}-r^{2}}{4s^{2}}
  =\frac{(15d)^{2}-(14d)^{2}}{4\,(15d)^{2}}
  =\frac{225d^{2}-196d^{2}}{900d^{2}}
  =\frac{29}{900}. \tag{22}
\]

The fraction \(\frac{29}{900}\) is already in lowest terms
(\(\gcd(29,900)=1\)), hence

\[
a.\text{num}=29,\qquad a.\text{den}=900. \tag{23}
\]

Finally

\[
a.\text{den}+a.\text{num}=900+29=929,
\]

which is exactly the desired conclusion (3). ∎
</informal_proof>
