\frac{35\pi}{72}= \frac{m\pi}{180}.
\]

2.4.  Cancel the common factor \(\pi\) and solve for \(m\):

\[
m =  \frac{35}{72}\cdot 180 = \frac{35\cdot 5}{2}= \frac{175}{2}.
\tag{5}
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 3.  Read off numerator and denominator

3.1.  The rational number \(\dfrac{175}{2}\) is already in lowest terms
(\(\gcd(175,2)=1\)).  Consequently, in the canonical representation of a
rational number,
\[
m.\text{num}=175,\qquad m.\text{den}=2 .
\]

3.2.  Adding them yields

\[
\uparrow m.\text{den}+m.\text{num}=2+175=177 .
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 4.  Conclusion

From the hypotheses \(h₀,h₁,h₂\) we have shown that the only rational
\(m\) satisfying them is \(m=\dfrac{175}{2}\); therefore the required
equality \(\uparrow m.\text{den}+m.\text{num}=177\) holds. ∎

