[2026-08-21 08:22:12,868][src.models.HILBERTWorker][INFO] - <informal_proof>
We prove the statement  

\[
\forall n\in\mathbb N,\;3\le n\;\Longrightarrow\; n!<n^{\,n-1}
\]

by ordinary (strong) induction on the natural number \(n\) starting at the
base value \(3\).

---

**1.  Base case \(n=3\).**  

\(3!\;=\;6\) and \(3^{\,3-1}=3^{2}=9\).  
Since \(6<9\), the inequality holds for \(n=3\).

---

**2.  Induction hypothesis.**  

Assume that for some arbitrary \(n\ge 3\) we have  

\[
\text{IH}:\qquad n!<n^{\,n-1}.
\]

Our goal is to prove the statement for the successor \(n+1\), i.e.

\[
(n+1)!<(n+1)^{\, (n+1)-1}=(n+1)^{\,n}.
\]

---

**3.  Rewrite the factorial of the successor.**  

\[
(n+1)! = (n+1)\cdot n!.
\]

---

**4.  Multiply the induction hypothesis by the positive number \(n+1\).**  

Because \(n\ge 3\) we have \(n+1>0\).  
Using the monotonicity of multiplication on \(\mathbb N\),

\[
\text{IH}\;\Longrightarrow\;(n+1)\cdot n! < (n+1)\cdot n^{\,n-1}.
\tag{4.1}
\]

---

**5.  Compare the right‑hand side of (4.1) with \((n+1)^{\,n}\).**  

Since \(n < n+1\) we may apply the monotonicity of the power function
(\(a<b\Rightarrow a^{k}<b^{k}\) for any positive exponent \(k\)) to obtain

\[
n^{\,n-1} \le (n+1)^{\,n-1}.
\tag{5.1}
\]

Multiplying (5.1) by the positive factor \(n+1\) yields

\[
(n+1)\cdot n^{\,n-1}\le (n+1)\cdot (n+1)^{\,n-1}
                = (n+1)^{\,n}.
\tag{5.2}
\]

---

**6.  Chain the inequalities.**  

From (4.1) and (5.2) we obtain

\[
(n+1)!\;=\;(n+1)\cdot n!
   \;<\;(n+1)\cdot n^{\,n-1}
   \;\le\;(n+1)^{\,n}.
\]

Thus  

\[
(n+1)! < (n+1)^{\,n}.
\]

---

**7.  Conclusion of the induction.**  

We have shown that the property holds for \(n=3\) (step 1) and that,
whenever it holds for an arbitrary \(n\ge 3\), it also holds for \(n+1\)
(step 6).  By the principle of mathematical induction, the inequality  

\[
n! < n^{\,n-1}
\]

holds for every natural number \(n\) satisfying \(3\le n\).

∎
</informal_proof>
