1. [THEOREM] Successor Step in Integer Induction from Base Point
Module: Mathlib.Data.Int.Defs
Name: Int.inductionOn'_add_one
Description: For any integer $z$ and base integer $b$ such that $b \leq z$, the induction step at $z+1$ is given by applying the successor case function $H_s$ to $z$, the proof $h_z$ that $b \leq z$, and the result of induction at $z$. That is, $(z + 1).\text{inductionOn}'\, b\, H_0\, H_s\, H_p = H_s\, z\, h_z\, (z.\text{inductionOn}'\, b\, H_0\, H_s\, H_p)$.
Signature:  (hz : b ≤ z) : (z + 1).inductionOn' b H0 Hs Hp = Hs z hz (z.inductionOn' b H0 Hs Hp)

2. [THEOREM] Integer Induction Principle
Module: Mathlib.Data.Int.Defs
Name: Int.induction_on
Description: Let $p$ be a property on integers. To prove that $p(i)$ holds for all integers $i$, it suffices to:
1. Show that $p(0)$ holds (base case),
2. For any natural number $i$, if $p(i)$ holds then $p(i+1)$ holds (positive induction step), and
3. For any natural number $i$, if $p(-i)$ holds then $p(-i-1)$ holds (negative induction step).
Signature:  {p : ℤ → Prop} (i : ℤ) (hz : p 0) (hp : ∀ i : ℕ, p i → p (i + 1)) (hn : ∀ i : ℕ, p (-i) → p (-i - 1)) : p i

3. [THEOREM] Base Case of Integer Induction from Base Point
Module: Mathlib.Data.Int.Defs
Name: Int.inductionOn'_self
Description: For any integer $b$ and properties $H_0$, $H_s$, $H_p$, the induction principle applied to the base case $b$ yields $H_0$, i.e., $\text{inductionOn'}(b, H_0, H_s, H_p) = H_0$.
Signature:  : b.inductionOn' b H0 Hs Hp = H0
