<justification>
The statement is correct.  
For any integer \(n\ge 3\) we have the hypothesis  

\[
n! < n^{\,n-1}.
\]

Since \(n+1 > n\) we obtain  

\[
n^{\,n-1} < (n+1)^{\,n-1},
\]

hence  

\[
n! < (n+1)^{\,n-1}.
\]

Multiplying both sides by the positive factor \(n+1\) yields  

\[
(n+1)\,n! = (n+1)! < (n+1)\,(n+1)^{\,n-1} = (n+1)^{\,n},
\]

which is exactly the desired conclusion.  
The argument uses only elementary monotonicity of the exponential function and the positivity of the factors, so the implication holds for every \(n\ge 3\). (In fact, the implication would be valid for any \(n\) for which the premise \(n!<n^{n-1}\) holds.)

Therefore the theorem is mathematically valid.
</justification>
