<justification>
The statement is correct.  
Assume \(n\ge 3\) and \(n! < n^{\,n-1}\).  
Then  

\[
(n+1)! = (n+1)\cdot n! \;<\; (n+1)\cdot n^{\,n-1}.
\]

Since \(n+1>0\), it suffices to show  

\[
n^{\,n-1} \;<\; (n+1)^{\,n-1},
\]

which holds because the base \(n\) is strictly smaller than \(n+1\) and the exponent \(n-1\) is positive (as \(n\ge 3\)). Multiplying the last inequality by the positive factor \(n+1\) yields  

\[
(n+1)\cdot n^{\,n-1} \;<\; (n+1)^{\,n},
\]

and chaining the inequalities gives  

\[
(n+1)! \;<\; (n+1)^{\,n}.
\]

Thus, given the premises, the conclusion follows, so the theorem is mathematically valid.
</justification>
