### Approach to the Lean Problem

The Lean problem says that for every integer \( z \), either \( f(z) = 0 \) or there exists a constant \( c \) such that for all integers \( z \), \( f(z) = 2z + c \).

But note that if there exists at least one integer \( z \) such that \( f(z) \neq 0 \), then the second alternative must hold for that \( z \), meaning that there exists a constant \( c \) such that for all integers \( z \), \( f(z) = 2z + c \). In particular, this would mean that the second alternative holds for all other integers as well. 

Therefore, the Lean statement is equivalent to saying that either \( f \) is identically zero, or it is of the form \( f(z) = 2z + c \).

Thus, perhaps we can first try to prove that either \( f \) is identically zero or it is of the form \( f(z) = 2z + c \), and then deduce the Lean statement from that. 

