[2026-08-21 09:56:28,848][src.models.HILBERTWorker][INFO] - The subgoal is mathematically correct! Full response:
[2026-08-21 09:56:28,848][src.models.HILBERTWorker][INFO] - YES
<justification>
The functional equation  

\[
\forall a,b\in\mathbb Z,\qquad f(2a)+2f(b)=f\bigl(f(a+b)\bigr)
\]

implies, by setting \(a=0\) and \(b=0\) respectively,

\[
f(f(b)) = f(0)+2f(b), \qquad  
f(2a) = 2f(a)-f(0).
\]

From these relations one obtains  

\[
f(2a)=2f(a)-c,\qquad\text{with }c:=f(0).
\]

If \(c=0\) the second equation gives \(f(2a)=2f(a)\); together with
\(f(f(b))=2f(b)\) this forces either \(f\equiv0\) or \(f(z)=2z\) for all
\(z\).

If \(c\neq0\) define \(g(z)=f(z)-c\).  Substituting into the original
equation yields \(g(2a)=2g(a)\) and \(g(g(b)+c)=2g(b)\).  The only
integer‑valued solutions of these are \(g(z)=2z\), i.e.  

\[
f(z)=2z+c\qquad(\text{for a fixed }c\in\mathbb Z).
\]

Thus any function satisfying the hypothesis is either the zero function
or an affine function of the form \(f(z)=2z+c\).  Consequently the
conclusion  

\[
\forall z,\; f(z)=0 \;\lor\; \exists c,\; \forall z,\; f(z)=2z+c
\]

holds: the outer universal quantifier together with the disjunction is
logically equivalent to “either \(f\equiv0\) or there exists a constant
\(c\) such that \(f(z)=2z+c\) for all \(z\)”.  Hence the theorem statement
is mathematically correct.
</justification>
