<justification>
The functional equation  

\[
\forall a,b\in\mathbb Z,\qquad f(2a)+2f(b)=f\bigl(f(a+b)\bigr)
\]

forces the function \(f\) to be either the zero function or an affine function of slope 2.  

* From the equation with \(a=0\) and \(b=0\) we obtain  
  \(f(f(x)) = f(0)+2f(x)\) and \(f(f(x)) = f(2x)+2f(0)\).  
  Equating the right‑hand sides gives  
  \(f(2x)=2f(x)-c\) where \(c:=f(0)\).

* Substituting this relation back into the original identity and using induction on the integer argument shows that the only possibilities are  

  * \(f\equiv0\), which indeed satisfies the equation, or  
  * \(f(x)=2x+c\) for some constant \(c\in\mathbb Z\).  Direct substitution verifies that every function of this form satisfies the functional equation.

Hence any function satisfying the premise must be either identically zero or of the form \(2x+c\).  The conclusion of the theorem is exactly this dichotomy, expressed as  

\[
\forall z,\; f(z)=0\ \lor\ \exists c,\;\forall z,\; f(z)=2z+c .
\]

The quantifier placement is logically equivalent to the intended statement “\(f\) is zero or there exists a constant \(c\) such that \(f(z)=2z+c\) for all \(z\)”.  Therefore the theorem is mathematically correct.
