Results for query: by_cases tactic for splitting a proposition into two cases
1. [DEFINITION] If-then-else splitting tactic
Module: Mathlib.Tactic.SplitIfs
Name: Mathlib.Tactic.splitIfs
Description: The tactic `split_ifs` decomposes all if-then-else expressions in the goal into separate subgoals. For a goal of the form `g (if p then x else y)`, it produces two subgoals: one assuming `p` and proving `g x`, and another assuming `¬p` and proving `g y`. When multiple if-then-else expressions are present, it splits them starting from the top-most expression whose condition doesn't contain another if-then-else. The tactic can also be applied to hypotheses using `split_ifs at *`, and allows custom naming of the introduced hypotheses with `split_ifs with h₁ h₂ h₃`.
Signature:  : Lean.ParserDescr✝

2. [DEFINITION] Case splitting tactic for inductive proposition equivalence
Module: Mathlib.Tactic.MkIffOfInductiveProp
Name: Mathlib.Tactic.MkIff.toCases
Description: The tactic `toCases` takes a metavariable `mvar` representing a proof goal and a list of `Shape` data structures. It introduces a new hypothesis `h` from the goal, performs case splitting on `h` to generate subgoals, and processes each subgoal according to its corresponding `Shape` data. For each subgoal, it filters relevant variables based on the `Shape` data, selects the appropriate subgoal, and either directly assigns a value or applies the `splitThenConstructor` tactic to further refine the proof.
Signature:  (mvar : MVarId) (shape : List Shape) : MetaM Unit

3. [DEFINITION] Cases tactic for inductive types
Module: Mathlib.Tactic.CasesM
Name: Mathlib.Tactic.casesType
Description: The tactic `cases_type` applies the `cases` tactic to a hypothesis `h` of the form `(I ...)` where `I` is one of the given inductive types. Multiple types can be specified, in which case the tactic will apply to any hypothesis matching any of them. The variant `cases_type*` repeats this process until no more applicable hypotheses are found, and `cases_type!` only applies the tactic if it results in at most one subgoal.

Example usage:
```lean
cases_type* Or And
```
This will destruct all conjunctions and disjunctions in the current goal.
Signature:  : Lean.ParserDescr✝

4. [DEFINITION] Pattern-matching case analysis tactic
Module: Mathlib.Tactic.CasesM
Name: Mathlib.Tactic.casesM
Description: The `casesm` tactic performs case analysis (`cases`) on hypotheses whose types match given patterns. It has several variants:
1. `casesm p` applies `cases` to any hypothesis `h : type` where `type` matches pattern `p`
2. `casesm p₁, ..., pₙ` applies `cases` to hypotheses matching any of the patterns
3. `casesm* p` efficiently repeats `casesm p` until no more matches are found
4. `casesm! p` only applies `cases` when it produces at most one subgoal

Example usage: `casesm* _ ∨ _, _ ∧ _` destructs all conjunctions and disjunctions in the current context.
Signature:  : Lean.ParserDescr✝

5. [DEFINITION] Strict pattern-matching case analysis tactic
Module: Mathlib.Tactic.CasesM
Name: Mathlib.Tactic.casesm!
Description: The tactic `casesm!` performs case analysis (`cases`) on hypotheses whose types match given patterns, but only when the case analysis produces at most one subgoal. It is a variant of the `casesm` tactic with stricter conditions on applicability.
Signature:  : Lean.ParserDescr✝

