|------|----------------------|-------------------|
| 3    | \(\text{Even}(d3)\leftrightarrow(\text{Even}(d2)\leftrightarrow\text{Even}(d0))\) | \(\text{Odd}(d3)\) |
| 4    | \(\text{Even}(d4)\leftrightarrow(\text{Even}(d3)\leftrightarrow\text{Even}(d1))\) | \(\text{Odd}(d4)\) |
| 5    | \(\text{Even}(d5)\leftrightarrow(\text{Even}(d4)\leftrightarrow\text{Even}(d2))\) | \(\text{Even}(d5)\) |
| 6    | \(\text{Even}(d6)\leftrightarrow(\text{Even}(d5)\leftrightarrow\text{Even}(d3))\) | \(\text{Odd}(d6)\) |

Thus we have the **parity pattern for the first seven indices**:

\[
\begin{array}{c|ccccccc}
n & 0 & 1 & 2 & 3 & 4 & 5 & 6\\ \hline
\text{Even}(d n) & \checkmark & \checkmark & \times & \times & \times & \checkmark & \times\\
\text{Odd}(d n)  & \times & \times & \checkmark & \checkmark & \checkmark & \times & \checkmark
\end{array}
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 3.  Periodicity of the parity sequence

Consider the *triple*  

\[
T_n :=\bigl(\text{Even}(d n),\;\text{Even}(d (n+1)),\;\text{Even}(d (n+2))\bigr).
\]

Because the recurrence (2) involves only the values at indices \(n-1\) and \(n-3\), the whole triple \(T_{n+1}\) can be computed from \(T_n\).  
There are only \(2^3=8\) possible triples; therefore the sequence \((T_n)_{n\ge0}\) must eventually repeat.

From the table of step 2 we have  

\[
T_0 = (\text{E},\text{E},\text{O}),\qquad
T_1 = (\text{E},\text{O},\text{O}),\qquad
T_2 = (\text{O},\text{O},\text{O}),\qquad
T_3 = (\text{O},\text{O},\text{E}),\qquad
T_4 = (\text{O},\text{E},\text{O}),\qquad
T_5 = (\text{E},\text{O},\text{E}),\qquad
T_6 = (\text{O},\text{E},\text{E}),\qquad
T_7 = (\text{E},\text{E},\text{O}) = T_0 .
\]

Hence **\(T_{n+7}=T_n\) for every \(n\)**.  
In particular, for each single index we obtain the period‑7 law

\[
\boxed{\;\text{Even}(d (n+7)) \;\leftrightarrow\; \text{Even}(d n)\;}
\qquad\text{and}\qquad
\boxed{\;\text{Odd}(d (n+7)) \;\leftrightarrow\; \text{Odd}(d n)\;}.
\tag{4}
\]

The proof of (4) proceeds by a straightforward induction on \(n\), using (2) to pass from the induction hypothesis for the three preceding indices to the statement for \(n+7\).

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 4.  Reducing the large indices modulo 7

Compute the remainders of the three numbers of interest:

\[
\begin{aligned}
2021 &= 7\cdot 288 + 5 \;\;\Longrightarrow\; 2021 \equiv 5 \pmod 7,\\
2022 &= 7\cdot 288 + 6 \;\;\Longrightarrow\; 2022 \equiv 6 \pmod 7,\\
2023 &= 7\cdot 288 + 7 \;\;\Longrightarrow\; 2023 \equiv 0 \pmod 7.
\end{aligned}
\]

Using the periodicity (4),

\[
\begin{aligned}
\text{Even}(d2021) &\;\leftrightarrow\; \text{Even}(d5),\\
\text{Odd}(d2022)  &\;\leftrightarrow\; \text{Odd}(d6),\\
\text{Even}(d2023) &\;\leftrightarrow\; \text{Even}(d0).
\end{aligned}
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 5.  Reading off the required parities

From the table of step 2 we already know  

* \(d5\) is **even**,
* \(d6\) is **odd**,
* \(d0\) is **even**.

Therefore  

\[
\text{Even}(d2021),\qquad \text{Odd}(d2022),\qquad \text{Even}(d2023)
\]

all hold simultaneously.  Consequently

\[
\boxed{\;\text{Even}(d2021)\;\land\;\text{Odd}(d2022)\;\land\;\text{Even}(d2023)\;}
\]

is proved.

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 6.  Formal justification of the tools used

* The equivalence (1) is exactly **Nat.even_add**.
* The equivalences **Nat.odd_iff_not_even** and **Nat.even_iff_not_odd** allow us to pass from “even’’ to “odd’’ statements.
* The fact that \(0\) is even and \(1\) is odd follows from **Nat.not_even_one** and the definition of parity.
* The case analysis on the remainder modulo 7 uses elementary arithmetic on natural numbers; no additional theorem is required.

Thus the theorem `amc12a_2021_p8` is established. ∎

