<justification>
The premise only states that the function \(f : \mathbb Z \to \mathbb Z\) is of the affine form  
\(f(z)=k\cdot z + c\) for some integers \(k\) and \(c\).  
No additional condition on \(f\) (or on \(k\) or \(c\)) is given. For any integer \(k\) we can define a function \(f\) of this form (e.g. choose any \(c\) and set \(f(z)=kz+c\)). Hence the conclusion “\(k = 0 \lor k = 2\)” does not follow from the hypothesis; it is false in general (e.g. take \(k=5\), \(c=0\) and \(f(z)=5z\)). Therefore the theorem cannot be proved from the supplied premises.

