The recurrence relation  

\[
d(n)=d(n-1)+d(n-3)\qquad\text{for all }n\ge 3
\]

is linear with constant coefficients.  Reducing it modulo 2 gives a linear recurrence over the field \(\mathbb{F}_2\):

\[
d_n \equiv d_{n-1}+d_{n-3}\pmod 2 .
\]

Its characteristic polynomial is  

\[
p(x)=x^{3}+x^{2}+1 .
\]

Over \(\mathbb{F}_2\) the polynomial has no root ( \(p(0)=1,\;p(1)=1\) ), so it is irreducible of degree 3.  In the field \(\mathbb{F}_{2^3}\) the multiplicative group has order \(2^{3}-1=7\); because the polynomial is irreducible, any root \(\alpha\) generates \(\mathbb{F}_{2^3}\) and thus has order 7.  Consequently \(p(x)\) is a primitive polynomial, and the sequence \((d_n\bmod 2)\) is purely periodic with period exactly 7 (or a divisor of 7, the only divisor being 1, which corresponds to the trivial all‑zero sequence).

Therefore for every \(n\),

\[
d_{n+7}\equiv d_n\pmod 2,
\]

which is precisely the statement  

\[
\text{Even}(d (n + 7)) \iff \text{Even}(d n).
\]

Since the argument uses only the given recurrence and holds for arbitrary initial values \(d(0),d(1),d(2)\), the theorem is mathematically valid.
</justification>
