<justification>
The hypothesis only imposes the relation  

\[
\forall x\in\mathbb Z,\qquad f(2x)=2f(x)-f(0).
\]

From this one cannot deduce the full “affine‑additivity’’  

\[
f(a+b)=f(a)+f(b)-f(0)\qquad(\forall a,b\in\mathbb Z).
\]

A counterexample can be constructed on \(\mathbb Z\).

Define \(c:=f(0)=0\).  
Choose arbitrary values for \(f\) on the odd integers; for instance set  

\[
f(1)=5,\qquad f(3)=7,\qquad f(5)=11,\dots
\]

Then define the values on even integers recursively by the given hypothesis:

\[
f(2x)=2f(x)\quad(\text{since }f(0)=0).
\]

Thus  

\[
f(2)=2f(1)=10,\; f(4)=2f(2)=20,\; f(6)=2f(3)=14,\dots
\]

One checks that the condition \(f(2x)=2f(x)-f(0)\) holds for every integer \(x\).

However the desired conclusion fails. For example, with \(a=1\) and \(b=2\),

\[
a+b=3,\qquad f(3)=7,\qquad f(1)+f(2)-f(0)=5+10-0=15\neq7.
\]

Hence the statement “if \(f\) satisfies the doubling identity then it satisfies the affine‑additivity identity’’ is false.

To make the theorem true one would need a stronger hypothesis, e.g. assuming that \(f\) is additive on a generating set (such as all integers) or that \(f\) is a group homomorphism up to a constant, or explicitly assuming the affine‑additivity property itself. With only the given doubling condition the conclusion cannot be proved.
</justification>
