while
\[
(f a-c)+(f b-c)=f(1)+f(3)=1+5=6.
\]

Thus \(f(a+b)-c\neq (f a-c)+(f b-c)\).  Hence the claimed identity does **not** follow from the single “double” condition.

The theorem could be made true by adding a suitable extra hypothesis, e.g. requiring that \(c = f(0)\) (so the identity becomes \(f(a+b)=f(a)+f(b)-f(0)\)), which holds for all affine functions \(f(x)=mx+f(0)\) that satisfy the given double‑argument condition.  Without such an additional assumption, the statement is false.
</justification>
